<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.3: Describing Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01370</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.3: Describing Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f9d51a47-d5aa-49cd-ab95-2c842807e928</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
      <para id="para-00001">Your teacher will give you a <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/404be7223239829b36370c118b70d9dac9e44f8b">set of cards</link> containing descriptions of situations and graphs. Match each situation with a graph that represents it. Record your matches and be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Use the cards to answer questions 1 – 4. Match each graph representation to the description of the situation characterized by exponential change. Then choose the relationship between the number of years since purchase and the value.</para>

      <section id="sect-00001">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="ee94e163-7f73-4cab-8d33-69086729ff4f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Choose the description of exponential change shown in the graph.</para>
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_06_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00004">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00009">Correct! A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Nice try! The correct answer is: A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00011">What is the equation for this graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00014"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00015">Where:</para>
          <para id="para-00016"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>value of the car after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years.</para>
          <para id="para-00017"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> initial price of the car.</para>
          <para id="para-00018"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> time in years.</para>
          <para id="para-00019"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> fraction by which the car loses its value every year.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_4f069820-3994-4438-ad4e-1d04aced19a6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00020">Choose the description of exponential change shown in the graph.</para>
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_06_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00021">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00022">The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00023">A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00026">Correct! The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Nice try! The correct answer is: The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00028">What is the equation for this graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00031">Where:</para>
          <para id="para-00032"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the current value of the stock after t years.</para>
          <para id="para-00033"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the initial value of the stock.</para>
          <para id="para-00034"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>is the number of years since the initial value.</para>
          <para id="para-00035">In this equation, we raise 3 to the power of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> to calculate the value after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years. This represents the stock tripling in value approximately every 8 years.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_425179bb-4b3d-40b6-b1e9-35c21f92dc6a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00036">Choose the description of exponential change shown in the graph.</para>
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_06_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00037">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00038">The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Nice try! The correct answer is: The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00044">What is the equation for this graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00045">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00046"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00047"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> value of the stock after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years.</para>
          <para id="para-00048"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> initial price of the stock.</para>
          <para id="para-00049"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> time in years.</para>
          <para id="para-00050"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> the number of doubling event for every four years.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
   
        <exercise id="_9a2c8284-2058-460c-a669-4671a60164b7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00051">Choose the description of exponential change shown in the graph.</para>
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_06_04.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00052">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00053">The value of a stock doubles approximately every 4 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00054">A car loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00055">The value of a stock triples roughly every 8 years.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00057">Correct! A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
            <para id="para-00058">Nice try! The correct answer is: A laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its value every year after purchase.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00059">What is the equation for this graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00060">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00061"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00062">Where:</para>
          <para id="para-00063"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>v</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the current value of the laptop after t years.</para>
          <para id="para-00064"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the initial purchase value of the laptop.</para>
          <para id="para-00065"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the number of years since the purchase.</para>
          <para id="para-00066">In this equation, the laptop loses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> (or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of its remaining value) every year. We raise <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> to the power of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to calculate the value after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Analyzing Graphs of Exponential Scenarios</title>
      <para id="para-00067">Watch the following video to learn more about how to analyze a graph to determine which exponential scenario it represents.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9381e45e777a0dff0ef45e7c94cae95307904b38"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00068">Which scenario could be represented by the following graph?</para>
      <para id="para-00069">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION THAT SHOWS TOTAL AMOUNT IN DOLLARS AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN MONTHS. THE FUNCTION HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF APPROXIMATELY 500.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
    </para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Connecting Exponential Situations and Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00070">Match each graph representation to the description of the situation characterized by exponential change.</para>
      <para id="para-00071">Graph 1</para>
      <para id="para-00072">
          <media alt="GRAPH 1 SHOWS AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 100 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (2, 400) AND (3, 800). ">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00073">Graph 2
  (<media alt="GRAPH 2 SHOWS AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 1 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (1, 5) AND (2, 25)."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_08_02.jpg"/></media>)</para>
      <para id="para-00074">Situation 1: A dangerous bacterial compound forms in a closed environment but is immediately detected. This bacteria is known to double in concentration in a closed environment every hour and can be modeled by the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><emphasis> </emphasis>is measured in hours.</para>
      <para id="para-00075">Situation 2: Loggerhead turtles reproduce every 2 to 4 years, laying approximately 120 eggs in a clutch. Using this information, we can derive an approximate equation to model the turtle population. As is often the case in biological studies, we will count only the female turtles. If we start with a population of one female turtle in a protected area and assume that all turtles survive, we can roughly approximate the population of female turtles by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00076">Since each graph represents exponential growth, and they both have the same labels, you have to look more closely at each. The biggest difference is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept or initial value. Graph 1 has an initial value of 100, and Graph 2 has a very small initial value. When you reread the situations, you can see that Situation 1 starts with 100 bacteria, and Situation 2 starts with 1 female turtle. This helps you see that Situation 1 belongs to Graph 1 and Situation 2 belongs to Graph 2.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Connecting Exponential Situations and Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00077">
          <media alt="Graph of function.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_08_03.svg"/>
          </media>
        
          <media alt="Graph of a decreasing exponential function, origin O. hours after taking medicine and mg of medicine in the body.Â ">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_08_04.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00078">Write a possible situation for each graph. Make sure to include vocabulary about exponential functions.</para>
            <para id="para-00079">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00080">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00081">Here is how to write an exponential situation when given a graph:</para>
            <para id="para-00082">First, determine if the function is growth or decay. If any points are provided, you can use them to determine the growth factor or initial value. Be sure to include the labels of the graph. Here are sample answers.</para>
            <para id="para-00083">First, graph the number of people infected growing exponentially each week.</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Second, graph the amount of medicine in the body starting at 80 mg and decaying by a factor of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> each hour.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
